yle of rapping that can  be jarring at first but once you get  into it man his one-liners are unmatched  at number seven i have the self-titled  album from laura stevenson  nothing creeps like measure time it's a  really fantastic mostly acoustic  singer-songwriter album i've been  listening to laura stevenson for a few  years now and i feel like this is the  most engaged i've been with one of her  albums and it's one i'm going to keep  playing for years number six is another  vinyl i haven't received yet and what  i'll have to put in in post but it is  bo jackson from boldy james and the  alchemist  again  i've already said my piece about  alchemist being one of the great  producers of our time but man bo jackson  there's a perfect match for him he's got  such a laid-back  ice cold delivery  and this is their second album they've  done together i don't know which i  prefer between this and the price of tea  in china but the work they do together  is just something else now we're in the  top five in the top five i gotta kick  off with home video from lucy dakis  [Music]  yeah i'll admit it i hadn't listened to  her music prior to boy genius and the  team up with fifi bridgers and julian  baker what a special album this is very  introspective very open and raw  emotionally a handful of my favorite  tracks of the year from this project  it's not one to be missed that number  four for me is tyler the creators call  me if you get lost  [Music]  that was personally one of my favorite  10 second reviews i did this year  and i love this project it is all over  the place in the best way it truly  embodies the globe trotting spirit but  also the spirit of mixtapes which is a  combination i don't think i've seen  before tyler's an artist who's  constantly evolving and exploring new  spaces and i really love the direction  he took on this project i can't wait to  see what's next at number three for me  is going to be jpeg mafia's lp  and i got to go with the offline version  j